Gandhi's insight that 'the means are the ends in the making' is empirically supported: revolutionary movements that use terror become terror states (French Revolution → Reign of Terror; successful movements using non-violence → democratic consolidation).
Research on institutional trust shows that states which use illegal means to achieve just ends (warrantless surveillance, torture) suffer lasting legitimacy damage that undermines the very democratic systems they claim to defend.
Psychological research on moral disengagement (Bandura) shows that people who allow ethical violations 'for good reasons' progressively expand the range of violations they will accept — the slope is demonstrably slippery.
